          "question": "What is a urinary tract infection, and why are they more common in women?",
          "answer": "A urinary tract infection (UTI) is a bacterial infection of the urinary system. Most involve the bladder and urethra, which is called a lower tract infection or cystitis. Infection that reaches the kidney is called pyelonephritis. It is a more serious illness, not just a worse version of the same one. *Escherichia coli* causes most cases. (Source: \"Urinary tract infection,\" General clinical reference, educational summary.)\n\nThe material I retrieved does not explain why UTIs are more common in women. I can't answer that part from these sources. A clinician or a reliable public health resource could explain it.\n\nIf you have symptoms or concerns about your own urinary health, please talk with a clinician. If you develop fever, back or side pain, vomiting, or feel very unwell, seek medical care promptly.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
